Q: How to best replace text using CSS only with restricted options A: <p>CSS cannot truly replace text content — it can only hide it and draw something else. With no JS, no HTML changes, and no font-size changes, the safest approach is to visually hide the original text while preserving layout, then overlay new text using a pseudo-element.</p>
<pre><code>h1 {
  color: transparent;
  position: relative;
}
h1::after {
  content: &quot;New Headline&quot;;
  color: inherit;
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
}
</code></pre>
<p>This keeps the original layout and typography intact, renders immediately, and is commonly used for CSS-only A/B testing.</p>

Q: TypeError: duplicate base class object A: <p>Base classes are different from constructor/initializer arguments. Both classes should simply inherit from <code>object</code> (though in Python 3 you don't need to do so explicitly. The place for one or more arguments is in the definition of <code>__init__</code> (or <code>__new__</code>, but we'll keep this simple):</p>

<pre><code>class Class1:
    def __init__(self, str1):
        ...

class Class2:
    def __init__(self, str1, str2):
        ...
</code></pre>

<p>If you do specify additional base classes, they must be <em>unique</em>.</p>

<pre><code># Illegal
class Class1(object, object):
    ...

# Legal, even though str also inherits from obj
# Don't actually do this, though.
class Class1(str, object):
   ...

# Legal: preferred version of the above
class Class1(str):
    ...
</code></pre>
